#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106712} :activity/id #uuid "bed445f2-ab2d-5551-bb75-6352876e2871" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050080 :topic/id #uuid "2bc94e96-3f59-5a1f-81f9-faaa1f8b7cb4" :topic/math-academy-id 1345} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107022 :tutorial/content "The area bounded by a polar curve $r = r(\\theta)$ and the rays $\\theta = \\theta_1$ and $\\theta = \\theta_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2\\,\\textrm d \\theta \\, .\n$$\n\nCalculating the integral is often straightforward if we know the integration limits $\\theta_1$ and $\\theta_2$. But what if we don't know these values?\n\nFor example, suppose we want to calculate the total area of the first petal of the curve $r=\\sin(3\\theta),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0c9059613162af33791e7edb7413dfbac9739cf24eeabd01b0033acba2b772a.png)\n\nThe curve bounding the blue petal crosses itself at the origin. When it passes through the origin, we must have $r=0,$ and since $r=\\sin(3\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin(3\\theta)=0 \\, .\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3\\theta = 0,\\pi \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = 0, \\theta_2 = \\dfrac\\pi 3,$ and the area of the first petal is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "774c3094-f23a-5a9b-90f7-90e89c6b5883" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1130 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c0856f04-60ee-5909-8802-83bd60dc8021" :step/math-academy-id 2223 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106717}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105791 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105646 :question/id #uuid "af255938-10ff-5169-9c5d-eac817354465" :question/math-academy-id "e-635"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c8ebd35-d0ad-5c62-a89d-7844a7a21587" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050106}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544862}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Sine Polar Rose in the First Quadrant"} :step/id #uuid "bc3630b3-b6c0-5729-9339-955c17b0c686" :step/math-academy-id 2226 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106821}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105792 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105647 :question/id #uuid "c4c49c0d-865a-55d1-8194-35dda3907269" :question/math-academy-id "e-636"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc2f214-e7a9-5bbc-9a83-241cca04ca07" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544860}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a General Polar Curve"} :step/id #uuid "2c17ef49-a79f-589f-ba64-7c3c729101b4" :step/math-academy-id 2228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105853 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105690 :question/id #uuid "3f355cb7-222f-5992-a212-f1b86ae37ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-9541"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5d5ccdb-070b-5ce1-b8af-dddb87675bdf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048625}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544839}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Cosine Polar Rose"} :step/id #uuid "7ace7e49-8905-52e0-be0e-4dd86bba8c36" :step/math-academy-id 35741 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106718}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107126 :tutorial/content "When working with polar coordinates, we primarily use the following domain for the polar angle $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nHowever, it's sometimes a good idea to use the domain \n\n$$\n-\\pi \\leq \\theta \\lt \\pi.\n$$\n\nChanging the domain for $\\theta$ turns out to be especially useful when a polar petal spans the first and fourth quadrants. \n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "529233d0-8f59-53b6-8be1-042c5b5ac84b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5842 :tutorial/title "Using an Alternative Range for the Polar Angle"} :step/id #uuid "7c91fbda-be5c-560d-bc46-59441d609e42" :step/math-academy-id 35801 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106819}}] :activity/title "Finding the Limits of Integration For a Given Polar Region" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105983}]}
